# key_identity.ledger — the BARE ENTITY-NAME KEY census.
#
# Held by tests/logos/key_identity_lint.sh. Read that file's header for what a
# subject is, how the population is derived, and what each cell means; this file
# is the pin, not the argument.
#
# ROW: <file> <TAB> <registry> <TAB> <cell> <TAB> <count> <TAB> <normalised key expression>
#
# Line numbers are DELIBERATELY absent: they drift with every edit above them,
# and a ledger keyed on them goes red for reasons that have nothing to do with
# this class. The key expression is the site's identity and also documents it.
#
# CELLS — two DERIVED (the ledger can only agree with the tree, never assert
# onto it), two demanding a ground comment AT THE SITE:
#   O     qualified probe FIRST, bare slot LAST. Derived: a package-qualifying
#         probe appears within the preceding 900 characters. Delete that probe
#         and the cell flips — RED.
#   Q     the key IS a mangled link name, qualified by construction. Derived.
#   D     bare by a carried design decision; needs `KEY-IDENTITY:` at the site.
#   B#nn  bare and reachable, a KNOWN OPEN DEFECT; needs `KEY-IDENTITY: OPEN #nn`
#         at the site with the SAME task number.
#
# ⚠ A NEW ROW IS NOT A FORMALITY. It says a lookup in the compiler answers for
# whoever registered a name first. Qualify the key (sema_key / qualify_pkg, or
# a keymaker grown from them such as mlir_struct_key) before you add one.
#
# ⚠ HELD IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. A REMOVED row is red too: a conversion is a
# deliberate ledger edit in the same commit, so the count can only move on
# purpose. A number that drifts down on its own is a measurement nobody reads.
#
# 41 rows / 45 sites. Cells: O 21, B#98 8, D 6, B#88 3, B#90 2, Q 2.
# (+1 O site 2026-08-22, #110: compute_auto_copy_types' is_copy_field enum arm
#  gained the qualified-then-bare `enums_` probe it never had — it used to ask
#  `struct_name()` (empty on an Enum) against the bare key, missed always, and
#  returned the generous `true`, classifying every enum-typed field as Copy.)
#
# ── ⚠ WHAT THIS LEDGER'S GREEN DOES **NOT** VOUCH FOR — `traits_` (#100) ────
# MEASURED 2026-08-22: `python3 key_identity_scan.py src/compiler
# include/logos/compiler | grep -c traits_` → **0**. Not one row. Yet `traits_`
# held THREE live consult defects, closed under #100:
#   · sema_decl.cpp lower_impl_block — a BARE `traits_.find(trait_name)` while
#     collect_impl used find_trait_iter_scoped, so the STDLIB homonym's
#     DEFAULT METHOD BODIES were lowered into a user impl and EMITTED as
#     unqualified globals (`T ZqH__is_empty` from a five-line user file).
#   · sema_impl.hpp check_trait_bounds_well_formed — a scope-aware lookup run
#     from a sweep with NO SCOPE (`cur_package_ = {}` two statements earlier),
#     so 27 of 127 stdlib trait names refused a user homonym on the stdlib
#     trait's arity.
#   · sema.cpp Fn-family — two bare-name intercepts, one over-refusing the
#     `dyn` spelling and one skipping the bound check entirely.
#
# WHY NONE OF THEM IS A ROW, stated precisely so nobody reads this as an
# oversight in the ledger: the SUBJECT is a key EXPRESSION produced by an
# entity-name PRODUCER call. These sites key on `trait_name` — a plain
# `std::string` local, populated far above from the AST. That is exactly the
# "KEYS LAUNDERED THROUGH A LOCAL" under-count the lint's own header records,
# and #100 is its first MEASURED instance rather than a hypothetical one. The
# header cites `impls_.count("StableLayout::" + n)` and adds "do not read the
# per-site counts as exhaustive"; read this as the same warning with a price
# attached — a defect class that ran the wrong CODE, invisible here.
#
# ⚠ AND THE `check_symbol_key_separators` EXEMPTION FOR `traits_` IS **NOT**
# RETIRED BY #100, DELIBERATELY. That exemption (sema_impl.hpp) is about the
# DOUBLE-SEPARATOR invariant — a stored key carrying two `::` makes find() and
# rfind() disagree — NOT about bare keys. Its stated ground is that traits_'s
# one `::` site, `resolve_trait_query_name`, is a `== npos` presence test that
# extracts no substring, and that is still true after #100's conversions (which
# only changed WHICH key is probed, never composed a nested one). Retiring it
# would be retiring a check of a different property on the strength of an
# unrelated fix.

# ── FACT 3 pins: the scan-by-name guards (no map, same class) ───────────────
# `callee == "<bare name>"` comparisons in sema_expr.cpp, by region. The two
# guarded regions are pinned so a comparison moving between them is seen; the
# third is the one that matters most — a bare-name intrinsic comparison written
# outside BOTH guards is the wrong-answer instance regrowing.
#GUARD sema_expr_callee_eq_in_lower_call_guard 14
#GUARD sema_expr_callee_eq_in_lower_type_intrinsic 66
#GUARD sema_expr_callee_eq_unguarded 11

# ── FACT 4: THE SCAN-BY-NAME POPULATION, PER FILE, WITH A ROSTER DIGEST ─────
# FACT 3 pins the two guards in sema_expr.cpp STRUCTURALLY. That is necessary
# and it is NOT sufficient: this round's own verify planted a bare-name
# intercept in sema_stmt.cpp and the gate stayed green, and the tree already
# held `if (name == "AnyVal")` in mlir_gen.cpp — a SILENT WRONG ANSWER of
# exactly the shape FACT 3 exists to stop, in a file FACT 3 does not read.
#
# So the population is censused tree-wide instead: <file> <count> <digest of the
# sorted literals>. A new intercept moves the count in whatever file it is born
# in; a literal swapped for another at a constant count moves the digest. The
# gate does not classify the site — that is the human's job at the pin move —
# it only refuses to let one appear unnoticed.
#
# ROW: #SCAN <basename> <count> <md5-8 of the sorted literal list>
#
# ── ROSTER RE-DERIVED 2026-08-22 (task #99), AND THE MATCHER WIDENED ────────
# The matcher above had a MEASURED blind spot: it read a name out of a VARIABLE
# or a METHOD (`t.type_str() == "X"`) but not out of a FREE FUNCTION
# (`type_str(recv_ty) == "X"`), which is how mlir-gen spells the same intercept.
# Over the HEAD tree the widening exposes EIGHT sites the gate could not see:
#   mlir_gen.cpp        1 -> 4   (+3: the three `type_str(...) == "AnyVal"`)
#   mlir_gen_expr.cpp   5 -> 8   (+3)
#   mlir_gen_fn.cpp     0 -> 1   (a file with NO row at all — unpinnable)
#   mlir_gen_stmt.cpp   0 -> 1   (likewise)
#   mlir_gen_types.cpp  1 -> 2   (+1)
# BITTEN: run the pre-fix mlir_gen.cpp past both matchers — old says
# `#SCAN mlir_gen.cpp 1 f40e89e1`, widened says `#SCAN mlir_gen.cpp 4 7fe89614`.
# The fourth of those four is `if (name == "AnyVal")`, which the OLD matcher did
# count (its LHS happens to be a variable called `name`) — that is how a file
# holding a live silent miscompile read green: the row was pinned at the one
# site that was visible.
#
# All eight are the same AnyVal/StringView representation channel and all eight
# are closed by this round, so the rows move DOWN, not up:
#   mlir_gen.cpp       RETIRED — it now holds ZERO bare-name intercepts. The
#                      `if (name == "AnyVal")` arm in gen_struct_lit is DELETED
#                      (measured: zero firings for its stated purpose over the
#                      whole tree) and the three `type_str(...) == "AnyVal"`
#                      comparisons now call the pkg-checked `is_anyval`.
#   mlir_gen_expr.cpp  5 -> 2. The two `.struct_name() == "StringView"` writ
#                      capture sites now call `is_stdlib_string_view`; the
#                      `strip_struct_pkg(tname) == "AnyVal"` spill test now
#                      compares the UNSTRIPPED mlir key. Remaining 2: `rint`
#                      (an LLVM intrinsic name, not an entity) and the
#                      unstripped `tname == "AnyVal"` — which is a QUALIFIED
#                      decision spelled as a bare literal, because the mlir key
#                      of the compiler's own un-packaged AnyVal IS "AnyVal"
#                      while a user's is "<pkg>.AnyVal".
#   mlir_gen_impl.hpp  1 -> 2. `is_anyval` and the new `is_stdlib_string_view`,
#                      both pkg-checked; the literal is the bare name they
#                      qualify, which is the intended shape.
# 2026-09-10f, THE `Copy` LANG-ITEM IDENTITY LANDING (nine bare-name intercepts
# CONVERTED, in the direction this ledger exists to encourage):
#   borrow_check.cpp  6 -> 4,  roster f7194ec0 -> 2e6b2e47. `impl.trait_name()
